WEBVTT

NOTE Captions for: What to do when your child answers in English

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
If your child understands you,

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:05.800
but answers in English
rather than the target language,

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.200
that doesn't necessarily mean
that the target language has failed,

4
00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:14.850
and understanding in the first place
is real progress.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.650
So instead of just right away
correcting them

6
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.550
or turning the conversation into a test,

7
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.720
you can answer them in the language
that you're supporting.

8
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.700
So repeat what they said
in that language

9
00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.350
and then try to keep
the conversation going.

10
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.420
So for example,
if your child is learning Russian

11
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.500
and says “apple,” you might answer:

12
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:41.950
<lang ru>Да, яблоко. Хочешь красное яблоко
или зелёное яблоко?</lang>

13
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:43.950
meaning, “Yes, an apple,

14
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.950
but do you want a red one,
or would you like a green one?”

15
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.450
And then over time,
you can invite your child

16
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.000
to try saying it themselves, like:

17
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.800
<lang ru>А как это будет по-русски?</lang>

18
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.750
So, “How do you say it in Russian?”

19
00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.050
As long as the conversation
stays playful,

20
00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.950
because overall, the goal
isn't to have perfect output.

21
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.100
What matters is just to keep
the conversation going in that language.
